abstract: >-
  A benchmark auditor that reads no pixels. Given only a label table — subject
  identifier, slice index, label, and split — it reports how much of a published
  performance number is reachable without any image data: from where a slice sits
  in its stack, from acquisition or release metadata, from volume size, or from
  prevalence alone. Each baseline is reported at both slice and patient level with
  subject-clustered bootstrap intervals, and calibrated against its own permutation
  null rather than against an assumed 0.5. Requires numpy and pandas only; no
  images, no GPU, and no data-use agreement covering pixel data.

  On the published label file and patient-disjoint split of a 2024 prostate
  diffusion MRI classification study, the positional baseline reaches 0.851
  (95% CI 0.816–0.887) slice-level AUROC against a reported 0.809–0.861, and falls
  to 0.424 (0.298–0.547) at the patient level.

  The construction is not novel and the package does not claim it: a position-only
  baseline was publicly disclosed in a 2020 Kaggle notebook ("Baseline with no
  image", RSNA-STR Pulmonary Embolism Detection), and a location-only baseline
  appears in DeepLesion's own defining paper (Yan et al., CVPR 2018). What is
  provided here is a reusable implementation with confidence intervals, the
  slice-versus-patient contrast, and permutation calibration.
